WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:08.364
The next morning I felt better. Joe and Biddy were very pleasant when I spoke of our

2
00:00:08.365 --> 00:00:11.547
approaching separation, but they only talked about it when I did. After breakfast Joe brought out my

3
00:00:11.548 --> 00:00:14.872
indenture papers. We burned them in the fire, and I felt that I was free. In the

4
00:00:14.873 --> 00:00:18.864
afternoon I strolled out alone to visit the marshes for the last time. As I passed the

5
00:00:18.865 --> 00:00:27.023
church, I felt great compassion for the poor creatures who were destined to go there Sunday

6
00:00:27.024 --> 00:00:32.356
after Sunday and then lie forever in the churchyard. As often happened when I walked through the

7
00:00:32.357 --> 00:00:36.578
churchyard, my thoughts turned to my miserable convict. I consoled myself by thinking that I had encountered

8
00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:41.015
him a long time ago. He had doubtless been transported a long way off and could even

9
00:00:41.016 --> 00:00:50.499
be dead by now. I continued on my way to the marshes. Lying down in the grass,

10
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.325
I fell asleep while thinking about whether Miss Havisham intended for me to marry Estella. When I

11
00:00:54.326 --> 00:01:00.309
awoke, I was very surprised to find Joe sitting next to me. He greeted me with a

12
00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:08.063
cheerful smile. "Since it's your last walk on the marshes, I thought I'd follow." "I'm glad you

13
00:01:08.064 --> 00:01:12.325
did, Joe." Then I told him that I'd always wanted to be a gentleman, and that

14
00:01:12.326 --> 00:01:17.309
I was sorry that he hadn't learned more when I tried to teach him. "It doesn't matter,"

15
00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:21.293
said Joe. But it did matter. When we had walked home and had tea, I took Biddy

16
00:01:21.294 --> 00:01:22.547
into our garden and asked a favor. "Promise me, Biddy, that you will take every opportunity to

17
00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:29.556
help Joe a little." "Help him?" "Joe is a dear, good fellow. But he's rather backward in

18
00:01:29.557 --> 00:01:34.460
some things, such as his learning and his manners." "Won't his manners do?" Biddy plucked a leaf

19
00:01:34.461 --> 00:01:37.230
from a black currant bush. "My dear Biddy, his manners do very well here—" "Oh, they do

20
00:01:37.231 --> 00:01:43.134
very well here ?" interrupted Biddy, looking closely at the leaf in her hand. "But if I

21
00:01:43.135 --> 00:01:48.697
were to remove Joe to a higher sphere, as I intend to do someday, they wouldn't serve

22
00:01:48.698 --> 00:01:54.944
him." Biddy rubbed the leaf to pieces and ever since then the smell of black currant

23
00:01:54.945 --> 00:02:02.951
has reminded me of our conversation. "Have you ever considered that he may be proud?" she asked.

24
00:02:02.952 --> 00:02:11.022
"He may be too proud to let anyone take him away from a job that he does

25
00:02:11.023 --> 00:02:18.102
well." "Now, Biddy, I'm very sorry to see you act like this. You are envious of my

26
00:02:18.103 --> 00:02:22.602
rise in fortune and you can't help showing it." "No matter what you think of me, you

27
00:02:22.603 --> 00:02:29.832
can be sure that I'll do everything that's expected of me here," said Biddy, and she went

28
00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:35.499
into the house. I went out at the garden gate and took a dejected stroll until dinnertime.

29
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.999
And once more I felt it was very strange and sad that this second night of my

30
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.086
bright future should be so lonely and unsatisfactory. But morning once again brightened my view, and I

31
00:02:44.087 --> 00:02:48.237
extended my forgiveness to Biddy and we dropped the subject. I went into the village and

32
00:02:48.238 --> 00:02:52.665
ordered some clothes from the tailor. I then went to see Mr. Pumblechook to ask if I

33
00:02:52.666 --> 00:02:58.300
might have my new clothes delivered to his house. He gave me lunch and congratulated me numerous

34
00:02:58.301 --> 00:03:05.134
times on my good fortune. I reminded him that we were not to mention the name of

35
00:03:05.135 --> 00:03:08.975
my benefactor. "And to think that I played a role in causing all this to happen," he

36
00:03:08.976 --> 00:03:14.888
said as he shook my hand. And so the week passed, and on Friday I went to

37
00:03:14.889 --> 00:03:19.650
Mr. Pumblechook's to put on my new clothes and then pay a call on Miss Havisham. I

38
00:03:19.651 --> 00:03:23.650
found her walking around the room that had the wedding cake on the table. "I leave for

39
00:03:23.651 --> 00:03:29.197
London tomorrow, Miss Havisham. I've come into such good fortune since I saw you last." I

40
00:03:29.198 --> 00:03:30.602
was very careful about what I said. "And I'm so grateful for it, ma'am." She waved her

41
00:03:30.603 --> 00:03:33.713
walking stick around me like a fairy godmother's wand. "Mr. Jaggers told me. So you are adopted

42
00:03:33.714 --> 00:03:35.261
by an unknown rich person?" "Yes, ma'am." "And Mr. Jaggers is your guardian?" "Yes, ma'am." "Well, you

43
00:03:35.262 --> 00:03:38.547
have a promising career before you." We talked a little more and then she said, "Good-bye, Pip!

44
00:03:38.555 --> 00:03:44.927
You will always keep the name of Pip, you know." "Yes, ma'am." She stretched out her hand

45
00:03:44.928 --> 00:03:46.356
and I went down on my knee and kissed it. I went back to Mr. Pumblechook's and

46
00:03:46.357 --> 00:03:50.110
put all my new clothes in a bundle. That night I dressed up in my new things

47
00:03:50.111 --> 00:03:53.927
to show Joe and Biddy. We had a hot supper and pretended we were happy, but we

48
00:03:53.928 --> 00:03:57.427
were all in very low spirits about my departure. I was to leave our village at

49
00:03:57.428 --> 00:04:03.078
five in the morning, and I had told Joe that I wished to walk away alone. I'm

50
00:04:03.079 --> 00:04:08.673
sorry to say that I didn't want anyone on the coach to see him with me. When

51
00:04:08.674 --> 00:04:13.403
I woke up, I smelled the kitchen fire and panicked, thinking that it was the late afternoon.

52
00:04:13.404 --> 00:04:18.570
But it was only Biddy making my breakfast. I got ready but couldn't go downstairs. I stayed

53
00:04:18.571 --> 00:04:24.253
in my room repeatedly unlocking and locking my suitcase until Biddy called to me that I was

54
00:04:24.254 --> 00:04:30.245
late. It was a hurried breakfast. I got up from the table and said, "Well! I suppose

55
00:04:30.246 --> 00:04:36.991
I must be off!" I kissed my sister and Biddy and threw my arms around Joe's neck.

56
00:04:36.992 --> 00:04:40.983
Then I picked up my little suitcase and walked out. I heard a scuffle behind me and

57
00:04:40.984 --> 00:04:43.904
looking back saw Biddy and Joe each throwing an old shoe after me for good luck.

58
00:04:43.905 --> 00:04:50.054
I stopped to wave my hat, and dear old Joe called out huskily, "Hooray!" while Biddy wiped

59
00:04:50.055 --> 00:04:57.126
her tears with her apron. I walked away at a good pace, thinking it was easier than

60
00:04:57.127 --> 00:05:04.459
I had supposed it would be. I whistled as I walked. But the village was very peaceful

61
00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:07.284
and quiet. Light mist was rising as if to show me how young and innocent I had

62
00:05:07.285 --> 00:05:14.864
been there and how everything beyond the village was so great and unknown. And at that moment,

63
00:05:14.865 --> 00:05:17.713
I broke into tears. When I was on the coach, I wondered with an aching heart if

64
00:05:17.714 --> 00:05:24.443
I should get down when we changed horses and walk back for another evening at home. But

65
00:05:24.444 --> 00:05:27.618
I couldn't make up my mind, so I didn't get down. I told myself I could

66
00:05:27.619 --> 00:05:31.285
still do it when we changed horses again. And while I was thinking about this, I thought

67
00:05:31.286 --> 00:05:39.094
I saw Joe walking along the road toward us and my heart rose. But it was another

68
00:05:39.095 --> 00:05:44.594
man. We changed horses again and yet again, and now it was too late and too far

69
00:05:44.595 --> 00:05:48.952
to turn back. And the mist had all risen now, and the world lay spread before me.

